  "faithful": "The rare ancient language of Ethiopians, which we shall now call by its modern name of Abyssinian, was according to that gentleman, the Geez, which was spoken by the ancient Cushite shepherds. This we should think approaches nearest to the old Chaldaic. Upon a revolution in Haheals, the court resided many years in the province of Anhara, where the people spoke a different language, or at least a very different dialect of the same language. During this interval, the Geez, or language of the subjects, was dropped, and retained only in writing, as a dead language; the sacred Scriptures being in that tongue and saved it from going into disuse. This dialect is exceeding harsh and unharmonious. It is full of the letters, D and T, on which an accent is put that nearly resembles stammering. Considering the small extent of sea which divides this country from Arabia, we need not wonder that it has great affinity with the Arabic. It is not difficult to be acquired by those who understand any other of the oriental languages; and as the roots of many Hebrew words are only to be found here, it seems to be absolutely necessary to all those who wish to obtain a critical skill in that language.",
  "modern": "The rare ancient language of Ethiopians, which we shall now call by its modern name of Abyssinian, was according to that gentleman, the Geez, which was spoken by the ancient Cushite shepherds. This we should think approaches nearest to the old Chaldaic. Upon a revolution in Haheals, the court resided many years in the province of Anhara, where the people spoke a different language, or at least a very different dialect of the same language. During this interval, the Geez, or language of the subjects, was dropped, and retained only in writing, as a dead language; the sacred Scriptures being in that tongue and saved it from going into disuse. This dialect is exceeding harsh and unharmonious. It is full of the letters, D and T, on which an accent is put that nearly resembles stammering. Considering the small extent of sea which divides this country from Arabia, we need not wonder that it has great affinity with the Arabic. It is not difficult to be acquired by those who understand any other of the oriental languages; and as the roots of many Hebrew words are only to be found here, it seems to be absolutely necessary to all those who wish to obtain a critical skill in that language."
